WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.172
So today, I'm going to show you how to use the CER model within

00:00:06.222 --> 00:00:07.950
our science class. And

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:10.450
this is something that's not just for science.

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:12.950
But it's for all areas of life, really.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:16.387
Sometimes you're going to hear it referred to as a “sir.”

00:00:16.438 --> 00:00:20.137
It means claim, evidence, reasoning. So if you hear “sir” or “CER”,

00:00:20.188 --> 00:00:22.950
you're thinking of the same thing there. Okay, so

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:27.547
CER (“sir”) stands for claim, evidence, reasoning, and it is how scientists explain their

00:00:27.597 --> 00:00:30.831
thinking and prove their ideas, and the cool part is,

00:00:30.881 --> 00:00:35.428
you'll be using it all the time, and I'll be showing you how this

00:00:35.478 --> 00:00:39.368
is something we use every day, not just in a science classroom,

00:00:39.418 --> 00:00:43.637
you'll find that you're using this type of thinking within your English class,

00:00:43.687 --> 00:00:44.950
within your history class.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:50.335
and really just out in the world, as you're observing things in the world

00:00:50.385 --> 00:00:55.335
around you. So this isn't just something that you're learning just for science.

00:00:55.385 --> 00:00:59.950
It's something that will help you in all areas of your life.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:04.617
And obviously, in this particular instance, we are looking particularly in our science classroom

00:01:04.667 --> 00:01:06.617
and with a scientific mind here.

00:01:06.667 --> 00:01:10.950
So in science, we always are going to start with an essential question.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:14.283
So it's a big question that makes you wonder, like,

00:01:14.333 --> 00:01:16.950
why do some objects sink while others float?

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:21.617
Or in today's example, we're going to use a silly example of why is

00:01:21.667 --> 00:01:23.950
there glitter all over the classroom floor

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:28.150
so they can be really big, essential questions, or it could just be something

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:31.450
in the world around you that you're asking a question about,

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:32.950
that. You're engaging in this.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:35.677
CER response sometimes not even in a formal way.

00:01:35.727 --> 00:01:39.920
But you're doing it in your everyday life, and we'll be talking about how

00:01:39.970 --> 00:01:42.950
we're going to do it in our science class here.

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:48.907
So sometimes scientists do background research in order to gather information before making a

00:01:48.957 --> 00:01:53.588
claim in our class. When you do your full CER assignments,

00:01:53.638 --> 00:01:59.546
you'll almost always start with research, like reading articles, watching videos or studying some

00:01:59.596 --> 00:02:02.950
sort of data. But today, in this example.

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:07.350
we are not going to be doing some background research.

00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:10.870
So everyday life. We're doing these CERs, really.

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:13.950
And we're doing stuff where we're noticing

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:17.379
what's happening, making observations and asking questions about it.

00:02:17.429 --> 00:02:21.950
So that's what we're going to be doing in this glitter example

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:26.739
for our model here, we're just making observations and inferences without that formal research

00:02:26.789 --> 00:02:29.476
piece. So when we get into science class.

00:02:29.526 --> 00:02:34.266
We're going to be combining those observations and those inferences with that background research

00:02:34.316 --> 00:02:38.371
to make our claims stronger. So in this example, like I said,

00:02:38.421 --> 00:02:41.792
we're not focusing as much on the background research part.

00:02:41.842 --> 00:02:46.582
But that is going to be a key piece as you move into these

00:02:46.632 --> 00:02:47.950
within our science classroom.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:51.820
So observations are what we notice about the world around us.

00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:55.339
They're things that we see, hear, smell, feel, or measure,

00:02:55.389 --> 00:03:00.265
and these things can be quantitative, meaning that they have a quantity attached to

00:03:00.315 --> 00:03:02.376
them, or they can be qualitative.

00:03:02.426 --> 00:03:06.950
Which means that they're talking about the qualities of that thing. So help.

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:10.266
if it's a big object or a small object.

00:03:10.316 --> 00:03:12.845
Maybe it's green, or maybe it's blue.

00:03:12.895 --> 00:03:18.003
Those are qualities versus like it is a 10 pound object that would be

00:03:18.053 --> 00:03:20.950
a quantitative value. So in this example, here

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:25.850
we have our picture of the glittered classroom with a student with glitter all

00:03:25.900 --> 00:03:30.750
over their hands, so the observations we could make are that there's glitter on

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:32.850
the floor near the art table.

00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:34.950
Okay, maybe there's an empty glitter

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:37.950
container near Sam's seat somewhere, like over here.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:42.825
There's an empty glitter container. Sam has glitter on his hands and shirt.

00:03:42.875 --> 00:03:46.950
Is Sam, with the glitter on the hands and the shirt.

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:51.075
So these are things that we are observing about this situation.

00:03:51.125 --> 00:03:54.825
When you go into these CERs in our science class,

00:03:54.875 --> 00:04:00.075
the background research is going to be articles and videos and different data that

00:04:00.125 --> 00:04:04.950
we're looking at. And we can make observations on those articles on that

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:09.950
video on that data. And that would be what we we would record here as we've

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:19.950
compiled our background research. That's where we do our observations and inferences from.

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:21.950
So from those

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:24.161
observations, we can make some inferences.

00:04:24.211 --> 00:04:27.845
And an inference is really just a smart guess, also,

00:04:27.895 --> 00:04:32.266
kind of like a hypothesis of what you think may have happened,

00:04:32.316 --> 00:04:35.950
based on those pieces of observation that you made. So

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:42.691
from our observations we have those inferences, and an example of an inference that

00:04:42.741 --> 00:04:48.950
we might make from this scenario would be oops went too far ahead

00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:51.950
would be that Sam might have spilled the glitter

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:56.617
because of the fact that it's near his seat, and there's glitter on his

00:04:56.667 --> 00:04:58.950
hands. Sam might have spilled the glitter.

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:02.929
The other thing could be that the glitter may have spilled during an art

00:05:02.979 --> 00:05:06.908
class. It looks like there might be some art supplies out glitter is an

00:05:06.958 --> 00:05:09.750
art supply. It probably spilled during some sort of class.

00:05:09.800 --> 00:05:12.592
And another possibility is that maybe the glitter bottle broke.

00:05:12.642 --> 00:05:16.571
So maybe they were using the glitter bottle and the lid just fell off,

00:05:16.621 --> 00:05:20.550
and it broke so that could be inferences based on the fact of all

00:05:20.600 --> 00:05:22.539
of these observations we made from above.

00:05:22.589 --> 00:05:25.950
So our inferences have to be based on something that we've observed.

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:29.950
Now that we have all those observations, and we were able to make some

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:33.950
inferences from them. Now we can make a claim which is which is really

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:35.950
our answer to that essential question. So

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:40.637
a claim is a statement that is answering that question.

00:05:40.688 --> 00:05:47.200
And an example for this would be that Sam spilled the glitter on the

00:05:47.250 --> 00:05:50.950
classroom floor. That's going to be our claim.

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:55.385
Our evidence are going to be the proof from the facts.

00:05:55.435 --> 00:05:59.821
So evidence comes from your observations and in formal science assignments,

00:05:59.871 --> 00:06:02.240
it also comes from that research.

00:06:02.290 --> 00:06:06.273
So in this example, we're using observations as our evidence.

00:06:06.323 --> 00:06:11.515
But in our class the observations are really coming directly from that research.

00:06:11.565 --> 00:06:15.950
The observations are the research. So the example here would be

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:20.604
from the evidence. Sam has glitter on his hands and shirt,

00:06:20.654 --> 00:06:23.988
and the glitter container was empty near Sam.

00:06:24.038 --> 00:06:26.950
So that's the evidence that we have.

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:29.400
Next, we go on to our reasoning.

00:06:29.450 --> 00:06:33.950
So our reasoning is going to tell basically why it makes sense. So

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:37.706
how does your evidence that you just provided prove that claim.

00:06:37.756 --> 00:06:39.755
So in this example we have.

00:06:39.805 --> 00:06:43.852
If someone spills glitter, it would get on their hands and clothes,

00:06:43.902 --> 00:06:47.950
and since Sam has glitter on him, it's likely he spilled it.

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:52.891
Now. The final step of writing your CER, is to combine it all together.

00:06:52.941 --> 00:06:55.009
So we have all these pieces.

00:06:55.059 --> 00:06:58.891
We've done all this research. And we've done all this thinking.

00:06:58.941 --> 00:07:02.421
Now, we need to have it in one concise area,

00:07:02.471 --> 00:07:05.950
so that we can share our findings with others. So

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:09.950
in this final summary paragraph you should start with your claim.

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:12.950
So Sam spilled glitter on the classroom floor

00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:18.283
next. Add your best evidence. There's glitter on his hands and shirt and the

00:07:18.333 --> 00:07:20.950
empty glitter container was near his seat.

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:23.521
And then, after that, explain your reasoning.

00:07:23.571 --> 00:07:27.195
So when someone spills, glitter, it often sticks to them,

00:07:27.245 --> 00:07:32.338
which explains why Sam is covered in glitter and then finally wrap it up

00:07:32.388 --> 00:07:35.644
with why it matters so based on the evidence.

00:07:35.694 --> 00:07:38.950
It is clear that Sam caused the glitter mess.

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:44.023
So remember that observations and inferences they are going to become your evidence,

00:07:44.073 --> 00:07:49.487
which you explain in your reasoning, and all of this supports your claim and

00:07:49.537 --> 00:07:51.828
comes together in your summary paragraph.

00:07:51.878 --> 00:07:54.950
So that's what we're saying right here. Okay.

00:07:55.000 --> 00:07:58.968
in our science class. We will be using these CERs for big questions.

00:07:59.018 --> 00:08:03.295
We'll do background research first. But in everyday life you're kind of doing this

00:08:03.345 --> 00:08:07.005
all the time in an informal way, not writing it all out.

00:08:07.055 --> 00:08:10.095
You're doing this process that we just did within this,

00:08:10.145 --> 00:08:14.114
CER, when you would have seen a glitter bottle spilled in a classroom,

00:08:14.164 --> 00:08:17.823
you would have engaged in this process without truly thinking about it.

00:08:17.873 --> 00:08:20.295
So it's something that you're already probably doing.

00:08:20.345 --> 00:08:25.550
And we are just putting some names to it and putting it in a more formal setting.
